  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height with contour lines. It is steeper along x, so overshooting starts there. Plain gradient descent diverges along x when the learning rate is above 0.8.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with standard deviation 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for each setting. That is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. A fixed seed sets it, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "The orange arrow is the actual step: −learning rate × the noisy slope. The grey arrow is −learning rate × the true slope. Both are drawn 2× their true length. The readout gives the true step length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the ball leaves the drawn bowl (diverged). Leaving the bowl is the plan's loss limit. Then the ball is drawn at the rim, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail marks every step, up to 40. 'New run, keep last path' keeps the finished path as the ghost and uses new noise values. Then press Run. While running, the ball takes 5 updates a second at 1× speed."
  ]
}